<Project>

    <!--
        THE ARCHITECTURE LEDGER — the policy the gate enforces, and the only place it is written.

        This is an MSBuild file rather than JSON for a measured reason: the gate runs as a
        RoslynCodeTaskFactory inline task, and that host cannot resolve System.Text.Json (verified — the
        compile fails with CS0234 on System.Text.Json) while it resolves System.Xml.Linq fine. Expressing the
        policy as MSBuild items means the ledger needs NO parser at all on the gate's side: MSBuild evaluates
        it and hands the task typed items. It also buys what JSON cannot hold — the reasoning below lives in
        comments beside the rule it justifies instead of in invented "why" string fields.

        `puck architecture` reads this same file as XML. One ledger, two consumers, nothing to drift.

        WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CONTAIN: which layer each project is in. That is declared per-project as
        <PuckLayer>/<PuckKind> in the csproj itself, and the gate reads those declarations. A central table
        of project-to-layer would be a second source for a fact the csproj already states, which is a place
        for the two to disagree and nothing else.
    -->

    <!--
        SCOPE — a stated predicate, not an incidental path filter.

        A QUARANTINED TREE IS OUTSIDE THE GATE, because quarantine means ungated. This has to be said out
        loud: the repository root's Directory.Build.props reaches experimental/Puck.Demo (nothing stops
        MSBuild's upward walk there), so without this the gate would run on a project deliberately removed
        from the graph and fail it against a ledger that cannot describe it. Puck.BareMetal is already immune
        for a different and better reason — it carries its own Directory.Build.props/.targets firewall pair
        that stops both discovery walks — and that immunity is a property of ITS build, never of this list.

        src/Web.Functions carries a second, unrelated exclusion below it: a separately built platform app that
        shares this tree, not part of the graph — never quarantined code awaiting retirement. It is gitignored
        and untracked, so it can carry no tracked Directory.Build.props/.targets firewall pair of its own the
        way Puck.BareMetal does, and the same upward walk that reaches experimental/Puck.Demo reaches it too.
        Puck.slnx never lists it, for the same reason.

        A filter that happens to exclude the right directories reads identically to one that excludes the
        wrong ones, which is why the reason sits beside the path.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureGateEnabled Condition="'$(PuckArchitectureGateEnabled)' == '' AND $(MSBuildProjectFullPath.Contains('$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)experimental$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)'))">false</PuckArchitectureGateEnabled>
        <PuckArchitectureGateEnabled Condition="'$(PuckArchitectureGateEnabled)' == '' AND $(MSBuildProjectFullPath.Contains('$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)src$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)Web.Functions$([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)'))">false</PuckArchitectureGateEnabled>
        <PuckArchitectureGateEnabled Condition="'$(PuckArchitectureGateEnabled)' == ''">true</PuckArchitectureGateEnabled>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        THE LAYERS, top row first. Rank is the row index: a dependency must point at an EQUAL or GREATER
        rank, so a same-row edge is permitted and an upward one is not. This ItemGroup is the source for the
        project map's layering block rather than a restatement of it: `puck architecture map` generates that
        block from these rows and each project's own declaration.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Composition roots" Rank="0" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Validation" Rank="1" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Engine services" Rank="2" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Presentation" Rank="3" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Backends" Rank="4" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Shared substrate" Rank="5" />
        <PuckArchitectureLayer Include="Leaf contracts and data" Rank="6" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        THE KIND AXIS — what a project IS, orthogonal to which row it sits on.

        A RANKED kind declares <PuckLayer> and obeys the rank rule at it. A TERMINAL kind must NOT declare
        one: it sits above every layer by construction, so a second declaration would only be somewhere for
        the two to disagree. Terminal kinds remain bound by every content rule below — a tool has no more
        business naming a GPU backend than an engine service does.

        A terminal kind is never a dependency OF A RANKED PROJECT. Terminal-to-terminal is fine, and that
        qualifier was earned rather than reasoned: stated flatly as "nothing may reference a terminal kind"
        the rule sounds exactly right, and the first run of the armed gate caught Puck.Analyzers.Tests
        referencing Puck.Analyzers as an ordinary assembly — a test suite that instantiates the analyzer and
        drives it over compilations it builds itself. The rule's purpose is that ENGINE CODE never depends on
        tooling; a test depending on the thing it tests is not that.

        Analyzer is worth its own row for a mechanical reason rather than a taxonomic one: an analyzer
        reaches every project through OutputItemType="Analyzer" with ReferenceOutputAssembly="false", so it
        never appears in a resolved reference set at all. The gate excludes it by construction, not by name —
        verified: Puck.Analyzers is absent from every project's @(ReferencePathWithRefAssemblies).
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Production" Ranked="true" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Validation" Ranked="true" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Test" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Tool" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="Analyzer" Ranked="false" />
        <PuckArchitectureKind Include="FileApp" Ranked="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        THE BACKEND QUARANTINE, narrow form — and the narrowness is the whole of its value.

        A project may hold a Backends-row assembly in its resolved closure only if
          (a) its layer is Presentation or Composition roots; or
          (b) its kind is TERMINAL and it does not name a backend directly — a terminal consumer inherits
              the closure of whatever it composes and never introduces one. That inheritance is safe by
              COMPOSITION rather than by trust: every ranked project in the closure has passed this same
              gate in its own build, so a backend arriving through one arrived legally; or
          (c) it carries an explicitly reasoned <PuckArchitectureBackendException> entry below. None do today.

        The wider form that suggests itself — "nothing may hold a Backends assembly unless it sits at or
        above that row" — permits any Engine-services project to take a direct backend dependency: legal by
        level, legal by closure, contradicting both the map's backend-neutrality rule and the entire reason
        the .Presentation wrapper row exists. That predicate would have flagged nothing, forever. Clause (b)
        is confined to terminal kinds for the same reason: extending closure-inheritance to ranked projects
        would reopen the hole through the .Presentation row, letting an engine service reach a backend by
        referencing a presentation wrapper.

        MEASURED, 2026-08-14: zero exceptions across the live graph. Puck.Post was the sole exception before
        its quarantine; experimental projects are now structurally outside this gate, so retaining that entry
        would be dead policy for an assembly the build does not produce. The absent finding remains load-bearing:
        the report proves no Engine-services project reaches a backend, rather than producing an empty sheet by
        widening the rule.
    -->

    <!--
        LANE PROFILES — exact-equality closures for the assembly split.

        The listed closure must EQUAL the project's resolved Puck closure, not merely contain it, so REMOVING
        an edge is as visible as adding one and the split's edit stays a reviewable diff.

        This is the only tier doing security work. Every forbidden edge in the split is SAME-ROW, so the rank
        rule cannot see one of them: Puck.Scripting, Puck.Commands and Puck.Input all sit on Shared
        substrate, and an edge between them is downward-legal and architecturally fatal. Never manufacture a
        rank difference so the cheap check can do the expensive check's job — the rows describe what may
        depend on what, and bending them to smuggle in a security boundary corrupts both rules at once.

        Generated against the SETTLED graph after unit 2 landed (f0a54cda), never from a snapshot taken
        mid-flight.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- The neutral channel/WASM core. Its whole purpose is the ABSENCE of Puck.Commands and
             Puck.Input; exact equality is what makes that absence enforced rather than merely current.
             Puck.Abstractions arrives through Puck.Assets' shared document-identifier contract. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Scripting" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Maths" />
        <!-- The field-as-data half of the SDF engine, split out of Puck.SdfVm: the instruction ISA, the packed-word
             program representation, the fluent authoring builder (including laying Puck.Text-authored strings out
             into marchable glyph geometry), and a warp-free deterministic fixed-point CPU interpreter
             (Puck.SignedDistance.Queries). Its whole purpose is the ABSENCE of every GPU/presentation/frame-
             production dependency Puck.SdfVm carries — exact equality enforces that a future member never quietly
             grows one. Puck.Abstractions arrives through Puck.Assets' shared document-identifier contract. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.SignedDistance" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Maths;Puck.Text" />
        <!-- The headless P1 split's document row (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Schema row; formerly
             Puck.World.Data). The exact-equality closure is wider than Puck.World.Schema's own direct references
             because Puck.World.Forge already needed Puck.HumbleGamingBrick and Puck.SignedDistance before this split
             existed, and Puck.HumbleGamingBrick in turn already needed Puck.Hosting — none of that is new here.
             Puck.Attestation: the admission handshake's identity door (WorldAdmissionDoor.cs) verifies a
             connecting peer's signed-attestation claim through it — Leaf contracts and data, same row as
             Puck.Maths/Puck.Storage, so no layer boundary moves. Puck.Physics is the production owner of
             deterministic collider vocabulary, contact geometry, and the body motion-program core; this project
             keeps the authoring vocabulary and its translation into that core. What this profile
             actually enforces is the ABSENCE that matters: no Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.SdfVm
             (the GPU renderer — this row reaches only Puck.SignedDistance, its field-as-data half), no
             Puck.Overlays, no Puck.Input, no Puck.World.Protocol, no Puck.World.Server. A member that would need
             one of those (the engine-default binding document, the live command/channel vocabulary check, the
             mutation-kind name catalog) crosses through an injection seam (BindingVocabularyHook,
             MutationKindVocabularyHook) the composition root wires with a module initializer — rather than a direct
             reference. Puck.Scripting is absent: WorldAddonRow carries no AddonLane, so nothing in this project
             names a Puck.Scripting type — Puck.World.Server (below) is the one row that still needs it, for the
             addon host itself. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Schema" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Maths;Puck.Physics;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge" />
        <!-- The world-agnostic wire substrate (docs/project-map.md's Puck.Networking row) — the socketless frame
             grammar and the bounded reader/writer pair every World socket frames its bytes through, plus the
             symmetric peer substrate (Puck.Networking.Peers: QUIC transport, identity-bound handshake, attested
             messages). Carries no World vocabulary, so its closure is exactly its two leaf dependencies: Puck.Maths,
             for the fixed-point scalar/vector/quaternion lanes the reader/writer read and write, and
             Puck.Attestation, for the signed claims a peer handshake and every peer message are verified as. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Networking" Closure="Puck.Attestation;Puck.Maths" />
        <!-- Sim-side music/rhythm state (MusicClock/MusicDirector/RhythmJudge) plus the presentation-adjacent
             fixed-point mixer/voice-synth core, bundled on one measured closure derived from what the moved
             mixer/synth files actually name (WorldMachineAudioRate relocated in, WorldVoicePatch.FromDocument's
             document intake left behind in Puck.World as WorldVoicePatchFactory) rather than a borrowed
             Puck.Physics precedent — Physics carries no lane profile and proves no closure of its own. Exact
             equality is what actually enforces the property that matters: Puck.Audio owns no world-document parser
             (and has no Puck.World.Forge edge) and holds no presentation/backend row. Puck.Assets is inherited from
             Puck.Commands because binding group identifiers use the shared document-identifier contract. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Audio" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Commands;Puck.Hosting;Puck.Maths" />
        <!-- The headless P1 split's wire-vocabulary row (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Protocol row; the residue
             of the former Puck.World.Data after Puck.World.Schema and Puck.Networking split out of it). Everything
             Puck.World.Schema's profile carries (Protocol references Schema) plus Schema itself and Puck.Networking
             (the frame/wire grammar its codecs frame payloads through). Same absence this profile enforces: no
             Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.Overlays, no Puck.Input, no Puck.World.Server. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Protocol" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Maths;Puck.Networking;Puck.Physics;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge;Puck.World.Schema" />
        <!-- The headless P1 split's server row (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Server row) — everything
             Puck.World.Protocol's profile carries (Server references both Schema and Protocol), plus Puck.Storage
             (the owned-world cloud sync substrate WorldOwnedWorldSync reads through). Puck.Attestation also arrives
             directly (WorldTcpHost/WorldTcpWireFormat frame the admission handshake's attestation bytes).
             Puck.AdvancedGamingBrick arrives for WorldScreenMachineEngines and Puck.Shaders for
             WorldPostRenderExtensions (the extension vocabularies every composition root's document validator
             checks a declared screen-machine engine/post-render extension key against — one static list, one
             shipped shader-set catalog) — the single source both Puck.World and Puck.World.Silo read, so a
             document that validates in one hosts identically in the other. No
             Puck.Scripting/Puck.Input: the addon guest host that needed them physically moved to Puck.World.Addons
             (below), which references this project rather than the reverse. Same absence this profile actually
             enforces: no Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.Overlays. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Server" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.AdvancedGamingBrick;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Audio;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Maths;Puck.Networking;Puck.Physics;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.Shaders;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Storage;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge;Puck.World.Protocol;Puck.World.Schema" />
        <!-- The presentation-facing client seam, split out of Puck.World (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Client
             row): the seat/roster surface, the editor, scene/session emitters, camera/view resolution, the stamp
             pool, and the SDF document intake. Everything Puck.World.Protocol's profile carries (Client references
             Protocol for the wire vocabulary and the relocated adjacency/grant-view/handle-table read surfaces),
             plus Puck.Assets, Puck.Audio (IWorldAudioFrameFeed/IWorldAudioCueSink narrow the frame/scene producers'
             AudioSnapshot/mixer-facing reads), Puck.Input (WorldEditorBindings), Puck.Launcher
             (PresentPacingControl), Puck.Overlays (the editor gizmo/HUD write surface), Puck.Scripting
             (WorldAffordances' command vocabulary), Puck.SdfVm (Views), and Puck.SignedDistance directly (Queries).
             No Puck.World.Server, no Backends row, no Presentation row: this project reads the document and the
             wire, never the authoritative fold. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Client" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Audio;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Input;Puck.Launcher;Puck.Maths;Puck.Networking;Puck.Overlays;Puck.Physics;Puck.Scripting;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge;Puck.World.Protocol;Puck.World.Schema" />
        <!-- The addon guest host, split out of Puck.World.Server so the authoritative server carries no
             WASM/Wasmtime surface of its own — everything Puck.World.Server's profile carries (Addons references
             Server directly), plus Puck.Scripting itself (the guest ABI AddonSimulationPump, hosted directly in
             this project, validates against) and Puck.Assets (the module bytes a mount reads). No Puck.Input: the
             source-id vocabulary AddonSimulationPump's predecessor once read moved to Puck.World, not here. Same
             absence enforced: no Backends row, no Presentation row, no Puck.Overlays. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Addons" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.AdvancedGamingBrick;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Audio;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Maths;Puck.Networking;Puck.Physics;Puck.Scripting;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.Shaders;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Storage;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge;Puck.World.Protocol;Puck.World.Schema;Puck.World.Server" />
        <!-- The control plane that follows the authority (docs/project-map.md's Puck.World.Console row):
             IWorldConsoleAuthority and the server-only console modules moved out of Puck.World (world.grant/
             .revoke/.grants/.why, world.groups/.ownership, world.looks/.population.spawn, world.market, world.peers,
             world.row.*/world.kits/world.assign, world.state.*/world.generate, world.update, world.wait). Everything
             Puck.World.Server's profile carries (Console references Server directly), plus Puck.Launcher (the
             ITextCommandHoldGate seam WorldConsoleWaitGate implements) and Puck.Input (Launcher's own dependency,
             not otherwise in Server's closure). Same absence this profile enforces: no Backends row, no Presentation
             row, no Puck.Overlays, no Puck.World.Addons — a module touching WorldClient/PlayerRoster/the seat
             surface/views/HUD/editor/screens/audio/recording stays in Puck.World instead of moving here. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.World.Console" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.AdvancedGamingBrick;Puck.Assets;Puck.Attestation;Puck.Audio;Puck.Commands;Puck.GamingBricks;Puck.Hosting;Puck.HumbleGamingBrick;Puck.Input;Puck.Launcher;Puck.Maths;Puck.Networking;Puck.Physics;Puck.SdfVm;Puck.Shaders;Puck.SignedDistance;Puck.Storage;Puck.Text;Puck.World.Forge;Puck.World.Protocol;Puck.World.Schema;Puck.World.Server" />
        <!-- The Linux platform split (docs/project-map.md's Puck.Platform.Linux row). PUCKARCH002 clause (a)
             already lets any Presentation-or-Composition-roots project hold a Backends assembly, so nothing stops
             a stray Puck.DirectX* reference from compiling here by rank alone — this exact-equality closure is
             what actually enforces the property the split exists to prove: a Linux build never links Windows or
             DirectX code. No equivalent profile for Puck.Platform.Windows — it legitimately carries the full
             Windows surface, so an exact-equality list there would only be a second place to keep that surface
             in sync, with no forbidden edge to police (Architecture.props's own reasoning above: lane profiles
             are "the only tier doing security work"). -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Platform.Linux" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Commands;Puck.Input;Puck.Maths;Puck.Platform" />
        <!-- The launcher family's OS-neutral member (docs/project-map.md's Puck.Launcher.Linux row). Same
             reasoning as Puck.Platform.Linux above: PUCKARCH002 clause (a) already permits a Presentation-row
             project to hold a Backends assembly, so only an exact-equality closure proves it never acquires
             Puck.DirectX/Puck.DirectX.Presentation/Puck.Platform.Windows — the property the owner's falsifier
             asks `puck architecture` to show directly, rather than by absence of a build error.
             Puck.Launcher.Windows gets no equivalent profile for the same reason Puck.Platform.Windows does not:
             it legitimately carries the full Windows surface. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Launcher.Linux" Closure="Puck.Abstractions;Puck.Assets;Puck.Commands;Puck.Hosting;Puck.Input;Puck.Maths;Puck.Platform;Puck.Platform.Linux;Puck.Shaders;Puck.Vulkan;Puck.Vulkan.Presentation" />
        <!-- The self-update stub (docs/project-map.md's Puck.Launcher.Stub row): its whole point is the ABSENCE of
             every Puck.* project, including Puck.Launcher itself — it references nothing but the BCL, so a bricked
             stub is unrecoverable in-band by construction rather than by policy, and this empty closure is what
             proves that rather than merely stating it. -->
        <PuckArchitectureProfile Include="Puck.Launcher.Stub" Closure="" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        The split's THIRD project — the presentation driver denied both Puck.Commands and Puck.Input — does
        not exist yet; it arrives with the four-surface retrofit. A profile for a project that does not exist
        cannot be verified and would be prose wearing a schema, so it is named here and becomes a
        PuckArchitectureProfile the moment the project lands.
    -->

    <!--
        INTERNALS-VISIBLE-TO, declared-vs-actual.

        The gate's declarations here are checked against each assembly's ACTUAL friend set read from the
        compiled PE, because scanning declarations trusts the thing being checked. Reading the PE is also the
        only form that sees both shapes this repository has used — [assembly: InternalsVisibleTo] in
        Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs, and <InternalsVisibleTo> csproj items — and a scan of either form alone
        returns a confident, wrong answer, measured by getting it wrong. Only the attribute form survives
        today; the PE read is kept because the csproj form costs one line to reintroduce.

        THE POLICY THIS TABLE ENFORCES: internals-visible-to is not endorsed here, and a TEST project is the
        only comfortable exception. Anywhere else, a member reached across a grant should be public instead —
        publicity is reviewable, a grant is a standing privilege nobody re-reads. So the shortness of this
        list is the point of it, and every row that is not a test project owes the reason it is not public.

        Puck.World HAD two friends — the file-based audio proofs — and they were the most privileged
        consumers in the tree, holding internals access to a composition root. They were also exactly what an
        analyzer-hosted gate could not have seen, since analyzer injection is off for $(IsFileBasedProgram),
        and a gate whose coverage silently excludes its most privileged consumers is worse than no gate. That
        argument is why the gate hooks where it does and is kept for it. Both grants are GONE as of the
        2026-08-02 quarantine: the scripts left the build, so the privilege was dead config that this ledger
        would have kept blessing. If those proofs are ever re-homed in a real project, the grant is re-issued
        to THAT assembly and named here — never restored to a file name.

        Gone with them: every grant naming Puck.Post (Puck.Maths, Puck.Platform and Puck.Scripting each held
        one), privilege pointing at an assembly the build no longer produces. Puck.Platform and
        Puck.Scripting held nothing else once Puck.Cli's grant became publicity on
        Puck.Scripting.WasmStdlibSources, so both AssemblyInfo.cs files are deleted rather than emptied and
        neither project appears below at all.

        Puck.Maths has one test-only friend, Puck.Maths.Tests, which reaches internal kernels to prove their
        individual rungs directly.

        Puck.Physics grants Puck.Physics.Tests its internal contact-solver state (the manifold slot table and
        speculative activation), so the ordering and contact laws can assert on solver internals the public
        surface deliberately does not expose. Test-only, like the Maths grant beside it.

        Puck.Networking grants nothing: its peer refusal tests drive an unsigned, wrongly-signed, or tampered
        message frame onto an otherwise-honest link through the public IPeerTransport seam (a test-side transport
        decorator hands the test the control stream), so no unattested-send member exists to befriend.

        Puck.Cli grants Puck.Cli.Tests its parity comparator internals (the manifest/contract loaders, the
        per-tile pixel comparer, the gate/state/pixel outcome model) — a CLI verb's own plumbing, never a public
        API. Test-only, like every other grant here.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Attestation" Friends="Puck.Attestation.Tests" />
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Cli" Friends="Puck.Cli.Tests" />
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Input" Friends="Puck.Input.Tests" />
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Maths" Friends="Puck.Maths.Tests" />
        <PuckArchitectureFriends Include="Puck.Physics" Friends="Puck.Physics.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
